## Why This Is Asked
Tests offline caching, data merging by id, and simple hook design for React Native with AsyncStorage.

## Key Concepts
- AsyncStorage usage for simple persistence
- useEffect/useState lifecycle and dependency on endpoint
- Merging lists by id while preserving user-visible order
- Exposing a small API (data, loading, error, refetch) for components

## Code Example
```javascript
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import AsyncStorage from '@react-native-async-storage/async-storage';

export function useCacheList(endpoint) {
  const [data, setData] = useState([]);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    const loadData = async () => {
      try {
        // Load cached data first
        const cached = await AsyncStorage.getItem(`cache_${endpoint}`);
        if (cached) {
          setData(JSON.parse(cached));
        }

        // Fetch fresh data
        const response = await fetch(endpoint);
        const freshData = await response.json();

        // Merge by id, preserving cached order
        const merged = mergeById(data, freshData);
        setData(merged);

        // Update cache
        await AsyncStorage.setItem(`cache_${endpoint}`, JSON.stringify(merged));
      } catch (err) {
        setError(err);
      } finally {
        setLoading(false);
      }
    };

    loadData();
  }, [endpoint]);

  return { data, loading, error, refetch: () => loadData() };
}

function mergeById(cached, fresh) {
  const map = new Map();

  // Preserve cached order
  cached.forEach(item => map.set(item.id, item));

  // Update with fresh data
  fresh.forEach(item => {
    if (map.has(item.id)) {
      map.set(item.id, item);
    } else {
      // Append new items
      cached.push(item);
    }
  });

  return Array.from(map.values());
}
```

## Component Example
```javascript
function EventsList() {
  const { data, loading, error } = useCacheList('/api/events');

  if (loading) return <ActivityIndicator />;
  if (error) return <Text>Error: {error.message}</Text>;

  return (
    <FlatList
      data={data}
      keyExtractor={item => item.id}
      renderItem={({ item }) => <EventCard event={item} />}
    />
  );
}
```

## Key Implementation
- Use FlatList instead of ScrollView for efficient virtualized rendering with large datasets
- Implement keyExtractor for stable item identification and optimal diffing
- Add getItemLayout to enable smooth scrolling by providing item dimensions upfront
- Configure initialNumToRender and maxToRenderPerBatch to balance performance and user experience

## Performance Optimizations
- Memoize renderItem components with React.memo to prevent unnecessary re-renders
- Use windowSize prop to control the render window and reduce memory footprint
- Implement image caching with react-native-fast-image for efficient image loading
- Consider removeClippedSubviews for memory optimization with complex lists
- Optimize data structure to minimize update calculations

